{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Azure Cosmos DB?

Azure Cosmos DB is a managed, globally distributed database service. An application
stores items in a **container**, groups them by a **logical partition key**, and pays
for database work in **request units (RU)**. Cosmos DB then maps logical partitions to
physical partitions, replicates them, and routes SDK requests to the configured regions.

It matters when a workload needs predictable database capacity, regional placement,
automatic partitioning, and an explicit consistency contract without operating its own
distributed database fleet. It does not remove the two hardest design decisions: which
key owns related data, and how stale a read may be.

{% callout variant="info" %}
**Core invariant:**

Every request spends RU inside one or more partition key ranges. A high account-level
RU budget cannot compensate for a low-cardinality key that concentrates traffic on one
physical partition.
{% /callout %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Items" eyebrow="Data" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
JSON records live in containers. Item ID plus partition key is the efficient address
for a point read.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Partition key" eyebrow="Ownership" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
The key groups related items into a logical partition and determines transaction and
query boundaries.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Request units" eyebrow="Capacity" icon="speed" tone="cyan" %}
RU normalizes CPU, I/O, and memory consumed by reads, writes, and queries.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Consistency" eyebrow="Read contract" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
The selected level defines freshness and ordering, while region distance and quorum
work shape latency and throughput.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Budget requests before provisioning capacity

A point read with the item ID and partition key is usually the cheapest read path.
Queries cost more as they inspect more items, predicates, projections, functions, or
partition key ranges. Writes also pay for item size and indexed properties. The SDK
response exposes the actual request charge; measure it instead of relying on one global
average.

The lab uses transparent teaching assumptions so you can separate two limits:

- **Aggregate pressure:** required RU/s divided by provisioned RU/s in one region.
- **Hot-partition pressure:** RU/s aimed at the busiest key range divided by that
  modeled physical partition's share.
- **Global capacity:** configured RU/s is available in every associated region, so
  region count multiplies the globally provisioned envelope.
- **Consistency cost:** strong and bounded-staleness reads use approximately twice the
  RU of relaxed reads for the same operation.

{% interactive-block id="technology/cosmos-db-performance" /%}

The model is not a price quote or benchmark. Calibrate it with request-charge headers,
normalized RU consumption by partition key range, item-size percentiles, index policy,
real queries, and a representative multi-region account.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Choose a partition key from access and growth invariants

A logical partition contains all items with one partition-key value. This creates a
useful atomicity and routing boundary, but it also creates a local storage and throughput
boundary. A good key has enough cardinality to distribute load and still appears in the
application's dominant reads, writes, and transaction groups.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Tenant or customer ID" eyebrow="Common fit" icon="users" tone="green" %}
Co-locates one tenant's state and makes tenant-scoped queries efficient. Split or use a
hierarchical key when a small number of tenants can dominate traffic or storage.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Status or country" eyebrow="Low cardinality" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
A few values collect most items and requests. Account metrics may look quiet while one
physical partition throttles.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Item ID" eyebrow="Point-access fit" icon="key" tone="amber" %}
Distributes writes and makes point reads efficient, but broader queries fan out because
the business grouping is no longer the partition boundary.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Trace the failure, not only the happy path

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/technology/cosmos-db/data/regional-partition-topology.json" /%}

Before creating a container, test candidate keys against:

1. the largest value's lifetime storage;
2. the busiest value's peak RU/s;
3. every atomic batch or stored-procedure boundary;
4. the dominant point reads and filtered queries;
5. growth from unusually large tenants, products, devices, or time windows;
6. migration options if ownership must change later.

The current [partitioning guidance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/partitioning)
documents logical and physical partitions, hierarchical keys, and common key
anti-patterns.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Select consistency from a user-visible promise

Read consistency applies to an individual read within a logical partition. Choose the
weakest level that still preserves the product invariant, then carry its client state
correctly. Lower network distance does not strengthen the selected guarantee.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Strong" eyebrow="Latest committed value" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Provides linearizable reads. Cross-region coordination raises latency, and read
throughput per RU is lower than relaxed levels.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Bounded staleness" eyebrow="Finite lag" icon="clock" tone="violet" %}
Bounds lag by versions or time. Use it when the product can state and verify a concrete
freshness window.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Session" eyebrow="Client continuity" icon="users" tone="green" %}
Provides read-your-writes and monotonic reads inside a session. Preserve and route the
partition-bound session token when work crosses processes.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Consistent prefix" eyebrow="Ordered history" icon="layers" tone="cyan" %}
Readers can lag but will not observe writes out of order. It does not guarantee a
maximum freshness delay.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Eventual" eyebrow="Convergent replicas" icon="network" tone="amber" %}
Prioritizes availability and low read coordination without ordering or freshness for
one read.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Per-request override" eyebrow="Narrow relaxation" icon="settings" tone="rose" %}
An SDK request can use a weaker read level than the account default. It cannot upgrade
a read beyond the account's configured write and replication contract.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

The official [consistency-level guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/consistency-levels)
defines the five guarantees and their quorum behavior. Recreate SDK clients after an
account-level consistency change so cached configuration does not outlive the rollout.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Follow the complete request path

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Address the item" label="Application" %}
Supply item ID and partition key for a point operation, or state explicitly that a
query may cross key ranges.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Route to a range" label="SDK" %}
Use cached account and partition maps, preferred regions, bounded retries, and the
session token required by the read contract.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Spend request units" label="Partition" %}
Execute the operation inside one physical partition or fan query work across several.
Return request charge and throttling metadata.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Replicate and observe" label="Service" %}
Apply the configured consistency and regional topology, then expose latency, RU,
availability, and partition-level pressure signals.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Prefer point reads when the address is known

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/cosmos-db/code/point-read.ts" language="typescript" title="Point read with request-charge telemetry" /%}

The item ID alone is not the full address in a partitioned container. Passing the key
lets the SDK route directly instead of broadcasting a query. Record request charge and
activity ID as operational metadata, but keep customer data and credentials out of logs.

### Make assumptions executable

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/cosmos-db/code/plan-request-envelope.py" language="python" title="Transparent RU and hot-key planning model" /%}

The script is deliberately small enough to review. Replace its illustrative RU weights
with measured request charges from the intended index policy, item sizes, and query set.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Provision and recover from measured demand

Choose one throughput mode from the workload, not from a generic preference:

- **Manual provisioned throughput** fits stable demand with a known envelope and an
  explicit scaling process.
- **Autoscale throughput** fits variable or unpredictable demand, but can still return
  `429` when total demand or one hot partition exceeds its available budget.
- **Dynamic autoscale** can scale regions and partitions independently for supported
  configurations; verify availability and account settings before relying on it.
- **Serverless** fits intermittent workloads within its documented limits; compare its
  economics and regional capabilities with the production contract.

Treat `429 Too Many Requests` as a capacity signal, not an invitation to retry without
a bound. Respect the SDK retry delay, cap end-to-end attempts, protect the caller's
latency budget, and separate expected RU saturation from hot-key concentration.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
**A regional account is not automatically a tested recovery plan.**

Availability depends on write-region mode, consistency, SDK preferred regions, retry
behavior, and the operation being attempted. Exercise region loss, stale topology,
session-token transfer, throttling, and ambiguous client timeouts with the exact account
configuration.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Operate Cosmos DB as a partitioned system

### Monitor together

- request charge and latency by operation, container, region, and status code;
- normalized RU consumption and throttled requests by partition key range;
- item size, index size, cross-partition query fan-out, and result cardinality;
- regional availability, replication behavior, SDK retries, and dependency latency;
- the business keys responsible for hot traffic without logging sensitive values.

### Drill deliberately

- one tenant or product becoming disproportionately hot;
- a traffic burst against a previously cold container;
- preferred-region loss and delayed topology refresh;
- an index-policy rollout that increases write RU or changes query behavior;
- a consistency change with old SDK instances still running;
- restore or migration into a container with a different partition contract.

### Review before release

1. Name the authority and atomicity boundary for every item family.
2. Measure point reads, writes, and representative queries with production-like items.
3. Prove the busiest logical key fits its storage and throughput envelope.
4. State the consistency promise in product language and test session propagation.
5. Bound retries and define user-visible behavior during sustained `429` responses.
6. Test regional routing and recovery rather than inferring it from the region count.
{% /section-card %}

